At 04:20 PM 6/17/02 -0700, Alan Womack wrote:
>A most likely misspelled attempt to say I don't have the sort of access to 
>the file system that is superuser status.

Yeah, I wasn't worried about misspellings ... I manage my own fair share of 
typos ... more about actual vagueness or ambiguity in what you report. For 
example ...

[...]
>What brought the problem to my attention is I wanted to run chkconfig 
>again and was unable to do so.
[...]

I'm not actually familiar with chkconfig ... I expect it's a Red Hat thing, 
and I use Debian ... but it illustrates the difference between the level of 
detail you provide and what we need. " ... was unable to do so" doesn't 
help us spot the problem; the actual error message that chkconfig (or maybe 
bash) reported could give us a clue about what is going on.

I'm not trying to nag here ... but you do mention your newness to Linux, so 
I want to help you understand the level of detail needed when asking about 
setup problems. Always tell us what you actually did, and quote (never 
paraphrase, until you get good enough that you *know* what the message 
means ... but then you won't need our help in interpreting it, right?) the 
exact result. As much as possible, try to avoid summarizing based on your 
guess as to what is important or what unfamiliar messages mean.


